Your Auth Service now exposes:

Method	API	Purpose
POST	/auth/register	Register client
POST	/auth/verify-mobile	Verify mobile OTP
POST	/auth/verify-email	Verify email OTP
POST	/auth/resend-mobile-otp/{userId}	Resend mobile OTP
POST	/auth/resend-email-otp/{userId}	Resend email OTP
POST	/auth/login	Login and receive JWT
Registration

Angular sends:

{
  "firstName": "Shubham",
  "middleName": "Raj",
  "lastName": "Bonde",
  "mobile": "9876543210",
  "email": "shubham@gmail.com",
  "tradingPlatformId": 101,
  "password": "Password@123"
}

Then:

Angular
   ↓
POST /auth/register
   ↓
AuthController
   ↓
AuthService
   ↓
Save User
   ↓
PENDING_VERIFICATION
   ↓
OTP Service
   ↓
Mobile OTP
Mobile verification
POST /auth/verify-mobile

{
    "userId": "USR-XXXXXXXX",
    "otp": "123456"
}

After successful verification:

mobileVerified = true
status = MOBILE_VERIFIED

Then Auth Service requests the email OTP.

Email verification
POST /auth/verify-email

{
    "userId": "USR-XXXXXXXX",
    "otp": "654321"
}

After successful verification:

mobileVerified = true
emailVerified  = true
status         = ACTIVE

Then the user is allowed to login.

Login
POST /auth/login
{
    "email": "shubham@gmail.com",
    "password": "Password@123"
}

Response:

{
    "success": true,
    "message": "Login successful",
    "data": {
        "accessToken": "eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiJ9...",
        "refreshToken": null,
        "tokenType": "Bearer",
        "expiresIn": 3600
    }
}
One dependency

Because AuthController uses:

@Valid

make sure your pom.xml has:

<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-validation</artifactId>
</dependency>

